# callable-contracts-unerased.bot -- tests/structural-contract-completions.test's
# scc-any-parameter-call in a program that erases nothing.
#
# apply calls its untyped parameter. Given the structural type
# Fn{args: [any, any], return: any} (no errors), apply fits only if nothing
# a call through that type may pass it can raise: a call through the type
# may pass it anything, so what apply raises through f is whatever the
# whole program erases (the erased-callable contract). In a program that
# erases an error-bearing callable that is a Type error
# (callable-contracts.bot's scc-any-parameter-call/erasing); in a program
# that erases nothing it fits.
#
# Why a suite of its own: the theorem is about a program with an empty
# erased-callable contract, and every other callable-contracts suite erases
# errors. The Tcl program is sccPrelude without its poison, plus the code
# below; the prelude's other functions, unused and erasing nothing, are left
# out (Job and quiet are the ones the program uses).
#
# Imports: compiler and test (every suite).
import compiler
import test

struct Job:
    run: Fn{args: [], return: int}

struct Apply:
    run: Fn{args: [any, any], return: any}

fn register_scc_unerased_tests():
    # scc-any-parameter-call/clean: accepted (value 0, the program's).
    fn any_parameter_call() errors compiler::CompilerError:
        static compile:
            fn quiet() -> int:
                7
            s = Job {run: quiet}
            fn apply(f, x):
                f(x)
            a = Apply {run: apply}
            0
    test::value("scc-any-parameter-call/clean", 0, any_parameter_call)

with context test::suite()
register_scc_unerased_tests()
test::report()
